// @check
// @error-at 26:5 field 'a' cannot be in a `flat` type: a string is a heap reference
// @error-at 27:9 field 'b' cannot be in a `flat` type: an array is a heap reference
// @error-at 28:5 field 'c' cannot be in a `flat` type: `boolean` has no declared byte width
// @error-at 29:11 field 'd' cannot be in a `flat` type: a nullable has no in-memory discriminant
// @error-at 30:14 field 'e' cannot be in a `flat` type: a function value is a reference
// @error-at 31:5 field 'f' cannot be in a `flat` type: `Plain` is a record but is not declared `flat`
// @error-at 32:14 field 'g' cannot be in a `flat` type: `{q:i32}` is a record but is not declared `flat`
// A flat field must have a BYTE WIDTH. Every rejected type here is rejected for a
// representation reason the message names, because that is the fact the programmer
// has to work around (webcraft P1.2, `docs/internals/flat-records-design.md` §3).
//
// `boolean` is the interesting one, and it is rejected DELIBERATELY. VL's boolean
// is an i32 at the wasm level, so 4 bytes would be the internally consistent
// answer — but C's `bool` is 1 byte, this tier exists to match foreign layouts
// byte-for-byte, and a `boolean` field silently occupying 4 is a 3-byte drift in
// exactly the code whose value is byte-comparability. `flag: i32` is a one-word fix.
//
// It is also an instance of the standing rule behind every ruling in this feature:
// WIDENING A REJECT LATER IS BACKWARD-COMPATIBLE AND NARROWING AN ACCEPT IS NOT.
//
// The INVERTED CONTROL is `flat-field-type-rejects-control.vl`: the same
// declaration with `flat` deleted, which checks clean. Without it, a reject that
// fires because the program is broken for some other reason looks like a pass.
type Plain = { p: i32 }
flat type Bad = {
  a: string,
  b: i32[],
  c: boolean,
  d: i32 | null,
  e: (i32) => i32,
  f: Plain,
  g: { q: i32 },
}
